.\" Written from docs/index.html by go run ./tools/man; edit the manual, not this.
.TH CONN 1 "September 2026" "conn 0.6" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- every project and its processes, in one window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn ls
.br
.B conn restart
.br
.B conn \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
A terminal UI. It lists each open project's shells, dev servers and agents, and
marks the ones waiting for input. macOS and Linux. Processes keep running when
the window is closed.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
1-1.
\fBconn\fR is a terminal UI for working on many projects at once. It lists every
git repository under your project roots, what is running in each, and the shells
you have open there, and marks the rows that need you.
.TP
1-2.
conn is one window. The navigator runs down the left; the shell you are working
in fills the right. Other shells keep running out of view until you return to
them. Close the terminal and everything keeps running; run \fBconn\fR again and
it is where you left it.
.TP
1-3.
Install the release built for this machine:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ curl -fsSL https://conn.w0zro.com/install.sh | sh
.fi
.RE
.TP
1-4.
The script checks the download against the release's checksums, puts the binary
in \fB~/.local/bin\fR and the manpage under \fB~/.local/share/man\fR. \fBman
conn\fR is this manual.
.PP
.I "Table 1-1. Install script overrides"
.RS
.TP
\fBCONN_INSTALL_DIR\fR
where the binary goes
.TP
\fBCONN_MAN_DIR\fR
where the manpage goes
.TP
\fBCONN_VERSION\fR
a release other than the latest
.RE
.TP
1-5.
From source, with Go 1.25 or later:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ go install github.com/w0zro/conn@latest
.fi
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
conn runs on \fBtmux\fR and reads the process list with \fBlsof\fR. Both must be
installed; neither needs to be configured or understood. macOS ships lsof;
\fBbrew install tmux\fR. On Linux, install both from the package manager.
\fBdocker\fR is optional: with it, a project's containers are listed too
(Section 6).
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ conn
.fi
.RE
.TP
1-6.
There is nothing to set up. The list opens showing what is running, so on a
quiet machine it says \fBnothing running\fR. Press \fB.\fR to show every
repository, \fB/\fR to find one by name. conn looks under \fB~/projects\fR;
Section 9 points it elsewhere.
.SH THE NAVIGATOR
.TP
2-1.
The left column is a tree, at most four levels deep: \fBgroup\fR →
\fBrepository\fR → \fBsub\-project\fR → \fBprocess\fR. A group, a repository
and a sub-project are each a place: a row you can open a shell at. A repository
is any git repository under a root. conn skips \fBnode_modules\fR, \fBvendor\fR,
\fBtarget\fR, \fB.build\fR, \fBPods\fR, \fB.venv\fR, \fBvenv\fR, every directory
whose name begins with a dot, and whatever \fBskipDirs\fR names.
.TP
2-2.
A folder inside a root that holds two or more repositories is a group. It is a
place of its own: \fBs\fR opens a shell there, \fBr\fR runs its plan, \fBx\fR
covers everything beneath it.
.TP
2-3.
In a monorepo, a directory with a manifest is a sub-project and gets a row under
its repository. A manifest is any of \fB.conn\fR, \fBProcfile\fR,
\fBpackage.json\fR, \fBdeno.json\fR, \fBcomposer.json\fR, \fBgo.mod\fR,
\fBCargo.toml\fR, \fBGemfile\fR, \fBmix.exs\fR, \fBpyproject.toml\fR,
\fBpom.xml\fR, \fBbuild.gradle\fR or \fBbuild.gradle.kts\fR. Directories git
ignores are skipped; an untracked \fB.conn\fR still counts.
.TP
2-4.
Each place lists what is running in it, named by what you would call it: \fBnpm
run dev\fR, not \fBnode\fR. A row that listens carries its port, \fBnpm run dev
· :5173\fR; an agent's row carries its model; a shell a plan or a task started
is named for its entry. Dim rows have no shell of their own to go to: processes
started outside conn, and processes a shell's command started in turn, a
claude's tools, say. \fBenter\fR reaches as far as the shell above them. All are
listed and killable. The row that is conn itself says \fB(me)\fR.
.TP
2-5.
A row's mark says how its command ended, or that its process is stuck. An
agent's marks are in Section 5.
.PP
.I "Table 2-1. Row marks"
.RS
.TP
\fB✗\fR
in red: the command ended badly, or the process is stopped or hung. Beside the
name, what the run said of itself and how long ago: \fBtest · 3 failed · 3m\fR
.TP
\fB✓\fR
in green: the command ended well. \fBtest · 12 passed · 2h\fR
.RE
.TP
2-6.
While the navigator has focus, the pane beside the list shows what is known
about the row under the cursor. For a place: its branch, status, upstream and
last commit; its plan as a checklist, each entry up, down or ended and how; its
tasks and how their last run went. For a process: its arguments, uptime, cpu,
memory, state, ports and children, and beneath the facts the last of what its
shell has shown. For a container: its name, image, status and the last of its
logs. What a thing is doing can be read without entering it.
.TP
2-7.
The status line at the foot begins with \fBCONN\fR, then the mode (Table 2-2),
then anything conn has to say until the next key: a report, an error, a
confirmation, or that a newer release is out. What a chord says from a shell
shows there too, for a few seconds. The right corner names the kind of agent
\fBa\fR starts.
.PP
.I "Table 2-2. Modes on the status line"
.RS
.TP
\fBNAV\fR
the navigator has focus
.TP
\fBPROC\fR
a shell has focus
.TP
\fBPREFIX\fR
ctrl-space pressed, waiting for the next key
.TP
\fBCOPY\fR
scrolling back through a pane
.TP
\fB/query\fR
a query being typed, or the filter standing
.TP
\fBCONTINUE\fR
the picker of conversations is open
.TP
\fBNEW\fR
a project's path is being typed
.TP
\fBCONFIRM\fR
a kill waits on its second key
.RE
.TP
2-8.
The mouse works throughout: the wheel scrolls back through a pane, a drag
selects and copies on release, a click focuses the pane under it, and a program
that uses the mouse gets its own clicks and wheel. Hold shift to select with the
terminal's own selection. A program that copies for itself, vim's clipboard yank
for one, reaches the system clipboard.
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A click into the navigator focuses it, as \fBctrl\-space \-\fR does: the shell
beside it moves out of view.
.SH MOVING
.TP
3-1.
In the list:
.PP
.I "Table 3-1. Moving in the list"
.RS
.TP
\fBj k\fR
move; the arrows move too
.TP
\fBgg G\fR
top and bottom
.TP
\fBspace\fR
fold or unfold the selected node; a folded node shows +n for what it hides
.TP
\fB\-\fR
fold or unfold everything; unfolded rows also show pids, and a container's id
.TP
\fB.\fR
everything, or only what is running
.TP
\fBJ K\fR
focus the next and previous shell, in the list's order
.RE
.TP
3-2.
\fB/\fR finds. Each word of the query must appear in order inside what a row
says, not contiguously: \fBtsl\fR finds tressle-api, \fBmono api\fR finds the
api inside mono, and the letters that matched are lit. A place answers by its
name; a process by anything its row says: the command, its arguments, the plan's
name for it, an agent's name and model, a port. Processes that answer are listed
under their places, pruned to the branches that answer. An empty query lists
places alone.
.TP
3-3.
A search is a way of getting somewhere, so the keys that go somewhere work while
the query is still being typed. Acting ends the search; the filter holds, so the
subject stays listed. \fBesc\fR cancels the search and puts the cursor back
where it was. The query takes readline's editing keys: \fBctrl\-w\fR,
\fBctrl\-u\fR, \fBctrl\-h\fR, \fBctrl\-e\fR and the rest.
.PP
.I "Table 3-2. Keys while a query is typed"
.RS
.TP
\fB↑↓ ctrl\-p ctrl\-n\fR
move through what is left
.TP
\fBenter\fR
open a shell on a place; step into a found process's shell
.TP
\fBctrl\-r\fR
run the plan there
.TP
\fBctrl\-a\fR
start an agent there
.TP
\fBtab\fR
the next thing that needs you
.TP
\fBshift+tab\fR
back: the previous shell, or the list
.TP
\fBctrl\-x\fR
ask to kill what the cursor is on
.TP
\fBesc\fR
cancel the search
.RE
.TP
3-4.
\fBenter\fR on a shell's row, or \fBs\fR on a place, focuses a shell beside the
navigator. From then on the shell receives every keystroke but one:
\fBctrl\-space\fR, which reaches conn from any shell. \fBctrl\-space \-\fR
focuses the navigator again. \fB?\fR, in the navigator or after ctrl-space,
lists every key in a popup over the window; Appendix A lists them here.
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
The window is tmux underneath. Its usual prefix and key bindings are off,
\fBctrl\-space\fR is the only prefix, and your own .tmux.conf is not read.
Started inside another tmux, conn stops with a message rather than nest.
.TP
3-5.
\fBtab\fR at the list, or \fBctrl\-space enter\fR anywhere, goes to the next
thing that needs you: an agent waiting, a command that ended badly, a process
stopped or hung. Again continues around them. A held shell is entered, at its
prompt or transcript; a process conn only watches gets the cursor.
.TP
3-6.
\fBshift+tab\fR at the list, or \fBctrl\-space ctrl\-space\fR anywhere, returns
focus to where it was: the shell before this one, or the navigator when focus
came from it. Again returns. Before focus has moved at all, it is the list from
a shell and the cursor's shell from the list.
.SH SHELLS
.TP
4-1.
\fBs\fR opens your shell at the selected place, beside the navigator, focused. A
shell you enter moves in beside the navigator; when the navigator takes focus
again it moves out of view, where it keeps running, and the navigator has the
whole window. Each shell keeps 10,000 lines of scrollback; \fBscrollback\fR in
the configuration raises that.
.TP
4-2.
\fBn\fR makes a new project. The status line asks where, on a line prefilled
with the cursor's folder relative to its root, \fBw0zro/\fR for a cursor on a
repository in that folder, and the name goes on the end. Backspace the folder
away for the root itself; type another and it is made. A path from \fB~\fR or
\fB/\fR is taken as it is. \fBenter\fR makes the directory, runs \fBgit init\fR
in it and opens a shell there; the cursor lands on the project once it is
listed. A directory already there is left alone. \fBesc\fR cancels.
.SH AGENTS
.TP
5-1.
\fBa\fR starts an agent at the selected place, in a shell like any other: Claude
Code, unless the configuration's \fBagent\fR says otherwise. Claude Code reports
its state, and the navigator marks each instance:
.PP
.I "Table 5-1. Agent marks"
.RS
.TP
\fB⠇\fR
working
.TP
\fB◆\fR
blocked mid-turn: a permission prompt, a question
.TP
\fB●\fR
finished its turn; waiting for the next ask
.TP
\fB○\fR
idle since it started
.RE
.TP
5-2.
The ◆ is the one to answer first: answering it resumes work already underway.
The pane for a claude row names what it is waiting on, the last thing asked of
it, and what it says it is doing. \fBtab\fR, or \fBctrl\-space enter\fR from any
shell, goes to it (3-5).
.TP
5-3.
A conversation does not end when its instance exits; it is suspended, and can be
picked back up. \fBA\fR on a place lists what is suspended there, sub-projects
included and, for a group, its repositories, newest first: when each last moved,
its branch, and the last thing asked of it. Typing narrows the list, \fBenter\fR
continues the one under the cursor in a shell opened where it was had, \fBesc\fR
closes the list. A conversation an instance still carries is not offered.
.TP
5-4.
conn knows two kinds of agent: \fBclaude\fR and \fBollama\fR. \fB,\fR at the
navigator, or \fBctrl\-space ,\fR from any shell, moves \fBa\fR on to the next
kind, around the end, for every window until conn is restarted; the status
line's right corner names it. Ollama's rows are plain processes, unmarked. It
runs the model most recently pulled or run; with nothing pulled, \fBa\fR runs
\fBollama list\fR instead, and with the ollama server down the REPL says so.
\fBagentRuns\fR in the configuration names what a kind runs when its own choice
is not the wanted one.
.SH PLANS
.TP
6-1.
Working on a project usually means the same processes every time. A project
lists them in \fB.conn\fR, Procfile format, one name and command per line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dev:   npm run dev
api:   go run ./cmd/api
agent: claude
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 6-1. A plan"
.TP
6-2.
Without one, conn reads the project's own files, in this order:
.PP
.I "Table 6-1. Where a plan comes from"
.RS
.TP
\fB.conn\fR
every line is an entry
.TP
\fBProcfile\fR
every line is an entry
.TP
\fBpackage.json\fR
one entry: its \fBdev\fR script, else its \fBstart\fR script
.TP
\fBcompose.yaml\fR
one entry, \fBcompose: docker compose up\fR, since compose runs every service
and attaches to each; \fBcompose.yml\fR, \fBdocker\-compose.yml\fR and
\fBdocker\-compose.yaml\fR the same
.RE
.TP
6-3.
\fBr\fR starts the entries that are not running, each in a window named for it,
and puts the cursor on the first. It is a list to run, not a supervisor: nothing
restarts on its own. When an entry's command ends, its shell stays at the prompt
with the transcript, the row shows ✗ or ✓, the pane's checklist says how and
when it ended, and \fBr\fR starts it again beside the old shell. Running
anything by hand in that shell clears the mark.
.TP
6-4.
A project that runs its services with docker compose shows each service as a
row: named for the service, with the ports it publishes, \fBweb · :8438\fR,
under the \fBdocker compose up\fR that runs it when one is running in a shell,
and under the place itself when compose runs detached. \fBenter\fR on a service
opens a shell inside its container. Its pane shows the container's name, image,
status and the last of its logs. A service that died stays listed while the
project runs, with the cross and how long ago, \fBworker · 3m ✗\fR, and
\fBtab\fR goes to it; one that exited well shows the check. A service whose
health check is not passing shows that in place of its ports: \fBapi ·
unhealthy ✗\fR, or starting.
.PP
.RS
.nf
compose-demo
   app
     cache · :6390
     web · :8438
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 6-2. A compose project's services, under the compose that runs them"
.TP
6-5.
What runs outside every project is listed under \fBglobal\fR, at the foot of the
list, while there is anything to list: containers started by hand or from
elsewhere, and processes listening on a port from outside your projects, an
\fBollama serve\fR or a \fBbrew\fR postgres. The system's own listeners are left
out. \fBx\fR, \fBenter\fR and the pane work there as under a project; there is
no directory, so no shell opens there and no project is made.
.TP
6-6.
What \fBx\fR stopped, \fBr\fR brings back where it was: the services the compose
file declares that are down are started with \fBdocker compose up \-d\fR, and a
compose up running in a shell picks up their logs again. An entry \fBr\fR is
starting that runs compose itself brings everything up instead. A service that a
plan entry is named for is left to the entry.
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A plan is not a supervisor. An ended entry's shell stays at its prompt, with its
transcript, until \fBx\fR closes it or \fBr\fR starts the entry again beside it.
.SH TASKS
.TP
7-1.
\fBt\fR, \fBb\fR and \fBl\fR run the place's tests, build and lint as the
project itself defines them. What the project wrote down comes first: a Makefile
target, a justfile recipe, a Taskfile task or a package.json script named
\fBtest\fR, \fBbuild\fR or \fBlint\fR. Otherwise the ecosystem's own command.
Only a linter the ecosystem ships with is guessed at; a project on ruff or
eslint names it in a script. A project the guess is wrong for says so in its
Makefile, which conn checks first.
.PP
.I "Table 7-1. How a task's command is found"
.RS
.TP
\fBMakefile, justfile, Taskfile\fR
the target, recipe or task named for the task
.TP
\fBpackage.json\fR
the script named for the task, run with npm
.TP
\fBgo.mod\fR
\fBgo test ./...\fR, \fBgo build ./...\fR, \fBgo vet ./...\fR
.TP
\fBCargo.toml\fR
\fBcargo test\fR, \fBcargo build\fR, \fBcargo clippy\fR
.TP
\fBpyproject.toml\fR
\fBpytest\fR; \fBpython3 manage.py test\fR for a Django project
.TP
\fBGemfile\fR
\fBrspec\fR with a spec directory, else \fBrake test\fR
.TP
\fBmix.exs and the rest\fR
the ecosystem's own: mix, gradle, mvn, deno, swift, dune
.RE
.TP
7-2.
Each run is a shell named for its task, so its row shows ✓ or ✗, \fBtab\fR
goes to a failure, and the place's pane says how each task's last run went and
how long ago. The key again replaces a finished run rather than stacking another
beside it; a run still going is left to finish. \fBconn test\fR, \fBconn
build\fR, \fBconn lint\fR and \fBctrl\-space t\fR, \fBb\fR, \fBl\fR do the same
from any shell.
.TP
7-3.
conn reads the run's summary line as the tool prints it: go test, pytest, jest,
vitest, mocha, cargo, rspec, ExUnit, golangci-lint, tsc, ruff, eslint. A failed
run's row says \fBtest · 3 failed\fR, a passed one \fB12 passed\fR. Output conn
does not recognize leaves the row with pass or fail alone.
.SH STOPPING
.I "Table 8-1. Kill keys"
.RS
.TP
\fBx\fR
kill the selected process
.TP
\fBX\fR
kill it, everything under it, and what it started that has since detached
.TP
\fBx on an entry\fR
end the entry's command and its shell; \fBr\fR starts it again
.TP
\fBx on a container\fR
stop the container
.TP
\fBx on a place\fR
kill everything running in it
.TP
\fBx on a signalled row\fR
kill outright: SIGKILL to what has not gone
.TP
\fBR\fR
end conn and every shell it holds
.RE
.TP
8-1.
Every kill asks first: \fBx\fR, \fBy\fR or \fBenter\fR confirms, anything else
cancels. Processes get SIGTERM. The question offers the other signals: \fB9\fR
confirms with SIGKILL, \fBi\fR with SIGINT, for a server that only tears down on
ctrl-c, and \fBh\fR with SIGHUP, for a daemon that reloads on it. A signalled
row shows a red marker until the process is seen to have gone; the list never
claims an exit it has not seen. A process that has not gone in five seconds is
given up on, and the status line says so; \fBx\fR on it then, or on a row still
on its way out, asks to kill it outright with SIGKILL. Kills work on any listed
process, including ones conn did not start.
.TP
8-2.
Killing a shell that is still running a command signals the command first and
closes the shell once the command has gone, so a \fBdocker compose up\fR stops
its containers on the way out. \fBx\fR on an entry's row kills the entry, and
the question names it: \fBkill app?\fR The entry is then gone, and \fBr\fR
starts it again. An exit that \fBx\fR asked for shows no mark. \fBX\fR, and
\fBx\fR on an entry, also kill processes the command started that have since
detached from it; such a process is listed under the command while it runs, and
the pane says \fBorphaned\fR of it.
.TP
8-3.
A container is stopped by docker, which signals the process inside it and, after
a grace period, kills it. \fBr\fR brings the service back (6-6).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
9-1.
One JSON file, entirely optional, at \fB~/.config/conn/config.json\fR;
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is honored.
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  \(dqprojectsDirs\(dq: [\(dq~/projects\(dq, \(dq~/work/mono\(dq],
  \(dqscrollback\(dq: 50000,
  \(dqnavWidth\(dq: 34,
  \(dqskipDirs\(dq: [\(dqbazel-out\(dq, \(dqdist\(dq]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 9-1. A configuration"
.TP
9-2.
Every field has a default; a file that names none is the same as no file.
.PP
.I "Table 9-1. Configuration fields"
.RS
.TP
\fBprojectsDir\fR
the directory searched for repositories; ~ and $VARS expand (default
$HOME/projects)
.TP
\fBprojectsDirs\fR
several roots; when set, projectsDir is ignored. A root that only exists on
another machine is fine
.TP
\fBscrollback\fR
lines of scrollback each shell keeps. Raising it takes a restart: \fBR\fR, then
\fBconn\fR again, or \fBconn restart\fR (10-2) (default 10000)
.TP
\fBnavWidth\fR
the navigator column's width, 16–60 (default 30)
.TP
\fBtheme\fR
dark or light, to match the terminal; the navigator itself uses the terminal's
own colors (default dark)
.TP
\fBagent\fR
the kind of agent \fBa\fR starts, until \fB,\fR says otherwise: claude or ollama
(default claude)
.TP
\fBagentRuns\fR
what starting a kind runs, by name: {\(dqollama\(dq: \(dqollama run mistral\(dq}
.TP
\fBskipDirs\fR
directory names never searched, on top of the built-in list
.RE
.SH LEAVING, RESTARTING, UPDATING
.TP
10-1.
\fBq\fR in the navigator, or \fBctrl\-space q\fR anywhere, leaves conn and
returns you to the terminal. Everything keeps running: the shells, the plans,
the agents. Run \fBconn\fR again, from any terminal, and it is where you left
it. If the navigator's pane was closed, \fBctrl\-space \-\fR brings it back.
.TP
10-2.
\fBR\fR, or \fBctrl\-space R\fR from any shell, ends conn and every shell it
holds, after asking. \fBconn restart\fR, typed in a terminal outside conn, does
the same and then starts conn again; inside conn it stops with a message, since
the shell running it would go too. A restart is the way to a fresh start, and
what a raised \fBscrollback\fR needs to take effect.
.TP
10-3.
conn checks for a newer release once a day. When one is out, the status line
says so whenever nothing else is being said: \fBconn 0.6.0 is out · U installs
it\fR. \fBU\fR installs it and restarts the navigator as the new build; the
shells stay. With nothing newer known, \fBU\fR checks now and says what it
finds. Installing a new binary by hand works the same: the next \fBconn\fR picks
it up. A build from source is never told it is behind.
.TP
10-4.
conn also answers on the command line. From a shell inside conn, these act on
the directory the shell is in:
.PP
.I "Table 10-1. Commands"
.RS
.TP
\fBconn home [key]\fR
to the navigator, pressing key there
.TP
\fBconn shell [dir]\fR
a shell in dir, beside the navigator
.TP
\fBconn agent [dir]\fR
an agent in dir, beside the navigator
.TP
\fBconn kind\fR
the next kind of agent, for \fBa\fR and the agent chord
.TP
\fBconn run [dir]\fR
the plan of the place holding dir
.TP
\fBconn test [dir]\fR
its tests; \fBbuild\fR and \fBlint\fR the same way
.TP
\fBconn jump\fR
the next thing that needs you
.TP
\fBconn back\fR
back: the previous shell, or the navigator
.TP
\fBconn next, prev\fR
the next and previous shell
.TP
\fBconn keys [client]\fR
the key list, in a popup; \fBconn page\fR is the page inside it
.TP
\fBconn ls\fR
the held shells: pid, directory, name, exit; one per line
.TP
\fBconn restart\fR
end conn, after asking, and start it again
.RE
.TP
10-5.
\fBconn ls\fR is for scripts: the held shells, one per line, pid, directory,
name and exit, tab-separated. The name is empty for a shell opened by hand; the
exit is how the shell's command ended, empty while it runs. With conn not
running it prints nothing, which is not an error.
.SH KEYS
.I "Table A-1. In the navigator"
.RS
.TP
\fB↑↓ j k\fR
move
.TP
\fBJ K\fR
next · previous shell
.TP
\fBenter\fR
open
.TP
\fBtab\fR
the next thing that needs you
